From 1c5a722190d42e194eb56bada7cc32fd50a3deb3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:27:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] Video at its own rate with VRR, in the mode of
 the menus: no change of mode

Kodi changes the mode of the display to the frame rate of a video ("Adjust display refresh rate"), and the
display goes black for that change when the video starts and when it stops. A display with VRR starts a refresh
when a picture arrives, within its VRR range: the mode of the menus can stay, and each picture be shown at its
own time.

A setting, "Video at its own rate with VRR (no change of mode)" (System > Display, expert level, off by
default), shown where the output offers VRR (vrr_capable 1). VrrVideo.h decides, with nothing of Kodi in it:
only SDR and HDR10 that are known to be progressive and are not 3D; never Dolby Vision (native or in the
stream), HDR10+, HLG or a video whose kind is not told; the display's VRR range known (HDMI Forum block; AMD's
FreeSync block for amdgpu, which takes the VRR of an HDMI display from it alone); on NVIDIA, the mode of the menus
one that NVKMS gives VRR (50 Hz, 720 lines, 110 % of the VRR minimum) with "HDMI variable refresh rate (VRR)" on.
The rate is the smallest whole multiple of the frame rate that is 5 % above the display's VRR minimum and at or
under the rate of the menus: 23.976 fps goes at 47.952 Hz, each picture twice, to a display whose VRR starts at
40 Hz. Anything else changes the mode as before. CResolutionUtils::ChooseBestResolution() asks the window system
first and keeps the mode of the menus where it says yes.

While such a video is in full screen, CWinSystemGbm sets VRR_ENABLED and keeps the time of each flip one of three
ways, the first the driver offers: Media VRR of NVIDIA's Blackwell (NV_VRR_MEDIA, NV_VRR_MEDIA_TARGET: the display
engine keeps the rate, fractional, by itself); each flip held by the display engine until its time
(NV_FLIP_TIME_RAW_NS, NVIDIA from Turing on); or Kodi waiting for each time with clock_nanosleep() and then
committing (amdgpu). The three NVIDIA properties come with nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9076. The commits are
blocking, so that the render loop goes at the rate; CGraphicContext::GetFPS() gives the rate, and CVideoSyncGbm
makes the times of the flips the vblanks of Kodi's clock where Kodi times them (a refresh that the display adds
by itself does not move the clock). Every commit with a change of mode, native Dolby Vision, the end of the full
screen or of the video stops it, with VRR_ENABLED 0 in the next commit.

kodi.log says what the display declares at each start ("variable refresh: HDMI VRR 40 to 120 Hz; QMS not
declared; AMD FreeSync not declared": an LG TV changes them with its VRR, FreeSync and QMS settings), the
decision for each video, and how exact the pictures came out, every 30 seconds and at the end: the intervals
between the refreshes that showed a flip against the intervals asked for (p1, median, p99, how many over 1 ms,
the refreshes the display made by itself), and the vblank time of each flip against the time asked for it
(p1, median, p99; the median also holds the display's own delay from a flip to its vblank time). Where the
flips held by the display engine come out more than 2 ms short, the driver does not hold them: Kodi waits for
the times itself from then on, and says so.

Host test: tools/regress/kodi-host/vrr_video_test.cpp in LibreELEC (72 checks: the rates of the LG C5, the
NVKMS rule, every refusal, Media VRR targets, the schedule to a million slots, the measure); 15 mutations of the
header, each caught. Not tried on hardware.

The texts of "HDMI variable refresh rate (VRR)" that said Kodi never flips with VRR say now that it does only for
a video at its own rate.
